(function () {
window.AINOW = window.AINOW || {};
var __exports = AINOW['data/data-coding'] = AINOW['data/data-coding'] || {};
const codingPrompts = [

  {"id":"c-1","title":"Payment Service Refactor","prompt":"You are a senior backend engineer reviewing a pull request on a Node.js + PostgreSQL microservice that handles payment processing for a multi-tenant B2B SaaS platform. The code below has been flagged for a readability and maintainability pass before it ships to production. Review it against SOLID principles, identify any violation of the single-responsibility principle, flag magic numbers or strings that should be constants, point out any async/await patterns that could cause unhandled promise rejections, and rewrite the problematic sections with clean TypeScript. Produce a review comment for each issue in the format a GitHub PR reviewer would write, followed by a fully refactored version of the file.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["code-review","refactoring","typescript","node.js"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"c-2","title":"Django ORM N+1 Audit","prompt":"You are a senior Python engineer auditing a Django 4.2 REST API that is exhibiting slow response times on list endpoints. The engineering team suspects N+1 query problems in the serializer layer. Review the code pasted below: identify every place where a queryset is evaluated inside a loop or a serializer method, annotate each with the SQL it generates, rewrite each section using select_related or prefetch_related with an explanation of why each choice was made, and estimate the query count reduction for a response set of 100 objects. Output the audit as inline comments followed by the corrected code.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["code-review","django","python","database","performance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-3","title":"React Component Complexity Reduction","prompt":"You are a senior frontend engineer doing a code review on a React 18 component that has grown to 400 lines and handles form state, API calls, error display, and three conditional render paths in a single function component. Using the single-responsibility principle and React's composition model, decompose the component pasted below into smaller focused components and custom hooks. For each extracted piece, state its responsibility in one sentence. Use TypeScript 5 interfaces for all props. Show the final file tree of new files created and provide the complete code for each. Do not use any class components or legacy lifecycle methods.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["code-review","react","typescript","refactoring","clean-code"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-4","title":"Go Concurrency Pattern Review","prompt":"You are a Go engineer with 8 years of experience reviewing a concurrent data pipeline that processes webhook events from Stripe using goroutines and channels. The codebase is written in Go 1.22. Review the code below for: goroutine leaks (channels that are never closed), race conditions that would be caught by the Go race detector, improper use of sync.WaitGroup vs errgroup, and context cancellation not being propagated. For each issue found, explain the production failure scenario it could cause, then provide the corrected code with comments explaining the fix.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["code-review","golang","concurrency","backend"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-5","title":"SQL Stored Procedure Modernization","prompt":"You are a database architect modernizing a legacy SQL Server 2012 stored procedure that has been ported to PostgreSQL 15 but still uses cursor-based row-by-row processing, SELECT * in subqueries, and implicit type casting. The procedure runs a monthly billing calculation for 50,000 customer records and currently takes 4 minutes to execute. Rewrite it using set-based operations, CTEs for readability, explicit casts, and window functions where appropriate. After the rewrite, add EXPLAIN ANALYZE annotations showing which parts of the old query were slow and what the new version avoids. Target execution time should be under 15 seconds.\n\n[Paste the SQL query or schema]","tags":["sql","postgresql","refactoring","performance","database"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-6","title":"TypeScript Strict Mode Migration","prompt":"You are a TypeScript engineer migrating a 3-year-old Express.js API from JavaScript to TypeScript 5 with strict mode enabled. The codebase extensively uses any, untyped third-party library calls, and implicit returns from async functions. Paste a module below and I will: replace every any with a precise type or a documented unknown with a type guard, add return type annotations to all exported functions, create Zod schemas for every external data boundary (incoming request bodies, third-party API responses), and explain each change with a comment. Output the fully typed module plus a types.ts barrel file with all shared interfaces.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["typescript","refactoring","type-safety","express"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-7","title":"Functional Refactor of Imperative Loop Logic","prompt":"You are a senior JavaScript engineer at a fintech startup doing a code review on a data transformation module that processes CSV bank transaction records. The module uses nested for loops, mutable accumulators, and in-place array mutations throughout. Refactor the code below using functional programming principles: replace every for loop with map, filter, or reduce; eliminate all variable reassignment by using const and immutable data flow; extract pure helper functions for each transformation step; and add JSDoc comments explaining what each pure function does and what type it returns. The refactored code must produce identical output to the original for the same input.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["refactoring","functional-programming","javascript","clean-code"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-8","title":"API Controller Technical Debt Audit","prompt":"You are a principal engineer conducting a technical debt audit on a Laravel 10 PHP API controller before a team of three new developers joins the project. The controller handles user account management including registration, email verification, password reset, and profile updates — all in a single 600-line class. Audit the code below and produce a structured report with three sections: (1) Critical debt — things that will cause bugs or security issues within 6 months, (2) Moderate debt — things that will slow down feature development, (3) Low priority — style and naming issues. For each item include: location in the code, why it is debt, and the recommended fix with a code example.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["code-review","php","laravel","technical-debt","architecture"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-9","title":"Rust Error Handling Overhaul","prompt":"You are a Rust engineer reviewing a CLI tool written in Rust 1.77 that parses TOML configuration files and provisions cloud resources via the AWS SDK. The current code uses unwrap() and expect() throughout, panics on any IO error, and mixes the anyhow and thiserror crates inconsistently. Refactor the error handling architecture: define a unified AppError enum using thiserror with variants for ConfigParseError, AwsApiError, and IoError; replace every unwrap/expect with proper ? propagation or match handling; update main() to use anyhow::Result and pretty-print errors with context; and add a section explaining the difference between using anyhow for application code versus thiserror for library code.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["rust","error-handling","refactoring","cli"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-10","title":"Design Pattern Application Review","prompt":"You are a senior software engineer reviewing a notification system for a SaaS product that sends emails, SMS, Slack messages, and push notifications. The current implementation uses a large if/else chain in a single NotificationService class that checks notification type and calls different provider SDKs directly. The team wants to add two new channels next quarter. Refactor this using the Strategy pattern in Python 3.12: define a NotificationChannel protocol, implement a concrete class for each existing channel, inject strategies into the service, and show how adding a new channel requires zero changes to the service class. Include a UML-style ASCII diagram of the final class structure.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["design-patterns","python","refactoring","strategy-pattern"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-11","title":"Node.js Memory Leak Investigation","prompt":"You are a senior Node.js engineer on-call at 2 AM because a production Express.js API server is running out of memory every 6 hours and restarting. The service handles real-time WebSocket connections for a dashboard product with 800 concurrent users. Analyze the error output and code snippet below. Walk through a systematic root-cause analysis: identify the three most common sources of memory leaks in long-running Node.js processes (event listener accumulation, closure over large buffers, unreleased timer references), show how to use the --inspect flag with Chrome DevTools memory profiler to capture a heap snapshot, explain what a detached DOM tree or retained closure looks like in the heap snapshot view, and provide a corrected version of the code with the leak patched.\n\n[Paste the error message and stack trace]","tags":["debugging","node.js","memory-leak","performance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:50:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"c-12","title":"React Infinite Re-render Debug","prompt":"You are a React 18 specialist debugging a dashboard component that is causing an infinite render loop. The browser tab freezes within 3 seconds of the component mounting, and the React DevTools profiler shows thousands of renders per second. The component uses useEffect, useState, and a custom hook that fetches data from a REST API. Analyze the code and error output below. Explain the dependency array rules for useEffect that are most commonly violated, identify the exact line causing the loop, show the mental model for tracing render cycles by adding strategic console.log calls, provide the fixed code, and explain how the ESLint plugin react-hooks/exhaustive-deps would have caught this at development time.\n\n[Paste the error message and stack trace]","tags":["debugging","react","hooks","frontend"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-13","title":"PostgreSQL Deadlock Analysis","prompt":"You are a database engineer debugging intermittent transaction deadlocks in a PostgreSQL 15 database that backs a multi-tenant inventory management system. The deadlocks occur during concurrent stock reservation requests and cause HTTP 500 errors for roughly 2% of checkout attempts at peak traffic. Analyze the pg_locks output and application code below. Explain what a deadlock cycle is and how PostgreSQL's deadlock detector resolves it, identify which two transactions are acquiring locks in conflicting orders, show the specific SQL statements causing the cycle, and rewrite the transaction logic using SELECT FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED to eliminate the deadlock. Include a short explanation of why lock ordering matters.\n\n[Paste the error message and stack trace]","tags":["debugging","postgresql","database","concurrency","transactions"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-14","title":"Kubernetes CrashLoopBackOff Diagnosis","prompt":"You are a DevOps engineer diagnosing a CrashLoopBackOff on a Python FastAPI service deployed to a Kubernetes 1.29 cluster on AWS EKS. The pod starts, serves one or two requests, then crashes with exit code 137. The deployment uses resource limits of 256Mi memory and 0.5 CPU. Analyze the kubectl logs and deployment manifest below. Explain what exit code 137 means (OOMKilled), show how to use kubectl top pods to confirm memory usage, identify whether the issue is a startup memory spike or a gradual leak, provide corrected resource request and limit values with a justification based on the app's expected behavior, and show the kubectl commands to apply the fix and verify the pod stabilizes.\n\n[Paste the error message and stack trace]","tags":["debugging","kubernetes","devops","python","memory"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-15","title":"JWT Authentication 401 Debug","prompt":"You are a backend security engineer debugging a JWT authentication failure on a Next.js 14 API route that uses the jose library for token verification. Users are intermittently getting 401 Unauthorized responses despite logging in successfully minutes earlier. The tokens are RS256-signed with a 15-minute expiry and are stored in an httpOnly cookie. Analyze the error output and verification code below. Walk through every possible cause of a valid-looking JWT failing verification: clock skew between the auth server and API server, algorithm confusion between RS256 and HS256, incorrect JWKS endpoint caching, and cookie SameSite policy blocking in cross-origin requests. Provide a diagnostic checklist and the corrected verification code with explicit clock tolerance handling.\n\n[Paste the error message and stack trace]","tags":["debugging","jwt","authentication","security","next.js"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-16","title":"Python Async Race Condition Debug","prompt":"You are a Python 3.12 engineer debugging a race condition in a FastAPI service that processes file uploads and stores metadata in Redis before writing to S3. Under concurrent load (more than 10 simultaneous uploads), the Redis metadata occasionally shows a different file's S3 key — meaning data is being associated with the wrong record. The code uses asyncio and aioredis. Analyze the code below. Explain how shared mutable state in asyncio coroutines causes race conditions even in single-threaded async code (cooperative multitasking and yield points), identify the exact await expression where the context switch allows another coroutine to overwrite the shared variable, and rewrite the critical section using asyncio.Lock to serialize access. Include a diagram in ASCII art showing the interleaved execution timeline.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["debugging","python","asyncio","race-condition","fastapi"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-17","title":"Webpack Bundle Infinite Build Loop","prompt":"You are a frontend build engineer debugging a Webpack 5 + TypeScript 5 project where webpack --watch mode enters an infinite rebuild loop. The terminal shows a new compilation starting within milliseconds of each completed build, causing CPU to spike to 100% and making development impossible. The project uses ts-loader, css-modules, and a custom file-generation plugin that writes type definition files to the src directory during compilation. Analyze the webpack config and plugin code below. Explain why writing output files into the watched source directory causes a feedback loop, show how to use the watchOptions.ignored configuration to exclude generated files, provide the corrected webpack.config.ts, and add a comment explaining how to use webpack-bundle-analyzer to confirm the fix doesn't affect the output bundle.\n\n[Paste the error message and stack trace]","tags":["debugging","webpack","typescript","frontend","build"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-18","title":"Docker Container Networking Failure","prompt":"You are a DevOps engineer debugging a Docker Compose 3.8 setup where a Next.js frontend container cannot reach a FastAPI backend container despite both being on the same named network. The frontend shows ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED when calling the backend, but the backend container is healthy and responding to curl requests from the host machine. Analyze the docker-compose.yml and Next.js API route code below. Explain how Docker bridge networking resolves container hostnames, why using localhost inside a container refers to that container's own loopback interface (not the host), show the correct service name hostname to use, explain the difference between ports (host binding) and expose (internal only), and provide a corrected docker-compose.yml with inline comments explaining each networking decision.\n\n[Paste the error message and stack trace]","tags":["debugging","docker","networking","devops","containers"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-19","title":"iOS Swift Crash Thread Sanitizer","prompt":"You are an iOS engineer debugging a Thread Sanitizer warning in a Swift 5.10 app that uses Combine publishers to update a UICollectionView data source. The crash log shows a data race on the main thread and a background DispatchQueue simultaneously accessing the same array of view model objects. The app crashes reproducibly when the user scrolls quickly while a network response arrives. Analyze the crash log and Swift code below. Explain what a data race is at the memory level, why UIKit requires all updates on the main thread, show how to use DispatchQueue.main.async and Combine's receive(on:) operator to funnel updates onto the main thread, and rewrite the affected Combine pipeline with the fix applied. Include a comment explaining how @MainActor in Swift 5.5+ simplifies this pattern.\n\n[Paste the error message and stack trace]","tags":["debugging","swift","ios","concurrency","combine"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-20","title":"GraphQL N+1 Query Trace","prompt":"You are a backend engineer debugging GraphQL performance issues on an Apollo Server 4 + Prisma 5 API. A query fetching 20 blog posts with their authors and comment counts is generating 41 SQL queries instead of 3, causing the endpoint to take 1.8 seconds on a warm connection pool. You have access to the Apollo Studio trace showing resolver execution times and a Prisma query log. Analyze the resolver code and trace output below. Explain the GraphQL N+1 problem using the specific resolver chain in this schema, show how DataLoader batches and caches resolver calls within a single request, implement a DataLoader for the author and commentCount resolvers, and show the Prisma query log before and after to confirm the fix reduces queries to 3.\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["debugging","graphql","prisma","performance","backend"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-21","title":"Merge Sort Teaching Doc","prompt":"You are a senior software engineer teaching a bootcamp graduate their first week at a startup. Explain merge sort to them using a JavaScript implementation, starting with the analogy of sorting a physical deck of cards. Your explanation must cover: the divide-and-conquer mental model with a diagram in ASCII art, the recursive call stack traced step-by-step for the array [38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10], a clean ES2022 implementation with a comment on every non-obvious line, the O(n log n) time complexity with a visual-friendly breakdown of why n levels × n work per level equals n log n, and one common interview mistake candidates make when implementing the merge step. Format as a structured teaching document the junior dev can keep as a personal reference.","tags":["algorithms","sorting","javascript","teaching","cs-fundamentals"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-22","title":"Dijkstra for Delivery Route Optimization","prompt":"You are a software engineer at a last-mile logistics startup building a route optimization feature. The backend is Python 3.12 and the product needs to find the shortest delivery path between a depot and 8 customer locations modeled as a weighted directed graph where edge weights represent average travel time in minutes. Implement Dijkstra's algorithm from scratch using a min-heap (Python heapq): define the graph as an adjacency list using a typed dictionary, implement the algorithm with clear variable names and a comment for each logical step, trace the algorithm's execution for a sample graph of 5 nodes printed to stdout, analyze the time complexity as O((V + E) log V) and explain why the heap is essential, and show how the solution would change if edge weights could be negative (Bellman-Ford).","tags":["algorithms","graph","python","optimization","data-structures"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-23","title":"LRU Cache Implementation for API Gateway","prompt":"You are a backend engineer implementing an in-memory LRU (Least Recently Used) cache for a high-traffic API gateway written in TypeScript. The cache stores responses keyed by URL + query string hash with a maximum capacity of 1,000 entries and a TTL of 60 seconds per entry. Implement the LRU cache from scratch without using any third-party library: use a doubly-linked list + HashMap architecture for O(1) get and put operations, implement get(key), put(key, value), and evict() methods, add TTL expiry logic that removes stale entries on access, write a TypeScript generic class so it works with any value type, analyze why a simple Map with insertion order does not satisfy the LRU eviction requirement, and include five unit test cases using Vitest covering capacity overflow, TTL expiry, and cache hit ordering.","tags":["algorithms","data-structures","typescript","caching","system-design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-24","title":"Binary Search on Rotated Array — Interview Prep","prompt":"You are preparing a mid-level backend engineer for a system design + algorithms interview at a FAANG company next week. Walk them through the 'Search in Rotated Sorted Array' problem (LeetCode 33) in depth. Your walkthrough must include: why naive binary search fails on a rotated array with a concrete example, the key insight that one half of the array is always sorted and how to use that, a step-by-step trace through the algorithm for the input [4,5,6,7,0,1,2] searching for 0, a clean Python 3.12 solution with every conditional branch commented, an explanation of why this is O(log n) time and O(1) space, two follow-up variations the interviewer might ask (find the rotation pivot, handle duplicates), and common mistakes candidates make in the conditional logic.","tags":["algorithms","binary-search","interview-prep","python","problem-solving"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-25","title":"Trie for Autocomplete Search Feature","prompt":"You are a full-stack engineer implementing an autocomplete search feature for an e-commerce product catalog with 500,000 SKU names. The feature must return prefix matches in under 10ms. Implement a Trie data structure in TypeScript 5: define TrieNode and Trie classes with insert(word), search(prefix) returning up to 10 results, and delete(word) methods; add a frequency counter to each terminal node so popular products rank higher; implement a DFS traversal for collecting all words under a prefix node; analyze the memory tradeoff of a Trie vs a sorted array with binary search for this use case; and explain how you would scale this to a distributed system using Redis sorted sets with lexicographic range queries as an alternative approach.","tags":["algorithms","trie","typescript","search","data-structures"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-26","title":"Dynamic Programming for Subscription Pricing","prompt":"You are a backend engineer at a SaaS company implementing an optimal discount allocation feature. Given a list of N subscription tiers each with a price and a discount percentage, and a total budget B to distribute across up to K tiers, find the allocation that maximizes total discount value. This is a variant of the 0/1 knapsack problem. Implement a dynamic programming solution in Python 3.12: define the problem formally with the recurrence relation written as a math expression, implement the bottom-up DP table approach with a 2D array, trace through the algorithm for a small example (4 tiers, budget of 100), reconstruct the chosen items from the DP table (not just the optimal value), analyze the time and space complexity and show how space can be reduced to O(B) with a 1D rolling array, and explain when the greedy approach (sort by discount ratio) gives the optimal answer and when it doesn't.","tags":["algorithms","dynamic-programming","python","optimization","problem-solving"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-27","title":"Consistent Hashing for Distributed Cache","prompt":"You are a distributed systems engineer designing the sharding strategy for a Redis cluster that will cache session data for 5 million active users across 8 nodes. Implement consistent hashing in Python 3.12 to distribute keys with minimal remapping when a node is added or removed. Your implementation must: define a HashRing class with add_node, remove_node, and get_node methods using Python's bisect module, use virtual nodes (150 per physical node) to improve distribution uniformity, demonstrate with a simulation that adding one node remaps only approximately 1/9th of keys, compare this to naive modulo hashing which remaps approximately 7/8 of keys when a node is added, and explain how this algorithm is used in production systems like Apache Cassandra and Amazon DynamoDB.","tags":["algorithms","distributed-systems","python","caching","system-design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-28","title":"Heap-Based Real-Time Leaderboard","prompt":"You are a backend engineer building a real-time gaming leaderboard for a mobile game with 100,000 active players. The leaderboard must support three operations efficiently: update a player's score, get the top 100 players, and get a specific player's current rank. Design and implement this in Go 1.22 using a min-heap for the top-100 window and a hash map for O(1) score lookups. Your implementation must: define the data structures with Go structs and interfaces, implement the heap.Interface for the min-heap, handle the edge case where a player's new score is lower than their current score, analyze the time complexity of each operation (update: O(log 100) = O(1), getTop: O(100), getRank: O(n) with discussion of how a skip list or Redis ZRANGEBYSCORE solves the rank query), and write a benchmark test using Go's testing.B to measure throughput at 10,000 updates per second.","tags":["algorithms","data-structures","golang","real-time","system-design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-29","title":"Bloom Filter for Duplicate URL Detection","prompt":"You are a backend engineer building a web crawler for an SEO analytics product. The crawler visits millions of URLs per day and needs to avoid re-crawling duplicates. A hash set storing all visited URLs would consume 8 GB of RAM for 100 million URLs. Implement a Bloom filter in Python 3.12 as a memory-efficient alternative. Your implementation must: explain the probabilistic data structure concept including why false positives are possible but false negatives are not, implement a BloomFilter class using a bitarray and k independent hash functions, derive the optimal formula for bit array size m and hash function count k given n=100M expected elements and a 0.1% false positive rate, show that this uses approximately 200 MB instead of 8 GB, implement add(url) and might_contain(url) methods, and discuss the practical implication of false positives for a crawler (re-crawling 0.1% of pages is acceptable).","tags":["algorithms","data-structures","python","probabilistic","system-design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-30","title":"Rate Limiter — Sliding Window Counter","prompt":"You are a backend engineer implementing rate limiting for a public REST API that allows 100 requests per minute per API key. The product has 50,000 active API keys and the rate limiter must work across multiple API server instances. Compare and implement two approaches in Python 3.12 with Redis 7: (1) the fixed window counter approach — show why it allows up to 200 requests in a 2-second boundary window, (2) the sliding window log approach using a Redis sorted set — implement it with ZADD, ZREMRANGEBYSCORE, and ZCARD, show it correctly limits to exactly 100 requests in any 60-second window, analyze the memory cost of storing timestamps vs counters, and show the Lua script to make the check-and-increment operation atomic so it works correctly under concurrent requests across multiple servers.","tags":["algorithms","rate-limiting","redis","python","distributed-systems"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-31","title":"FastAPI Async CRUD with PostgreSQL","prompt":"You are a senior Python engineer building the backend for a project management SaaS. Design and implement a complete FastAPI 0.111 async CRUD API for a Project resource with PostgreSQL 15 via asyncpg and SQLAlchemy 2.0 async ORM. Your implementation must include: the SQLAlchemy model with created_at and updated_at auto-timestamps, a Pydantic v2 schema with validators ensuring project names are 3–100 characters and trimmed, all five endpoints (POST /projects, GET /projects with pagination using cursor-based pagination not offset, GET /projects/{id}, PATCH /projects/{id} with partial update, DELETE /projects/{id}), a dependency-injected async database session, proper HTTP status codes (201, 200, 404, 422), and a startup lifespan event that runs Alembic migrations. Show the Alembic migration file for the projects table.","tags":["backend","fastapi","python","postgresql","rest-api"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:30:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"c-32","title":"gRPC Service in Go with Protobuf","prompt":"You are a backend engineer building an internal user profile microservice in Go 1.22 that will be called by 12 other microservices via gRPC. Define and implement a UserService with four RPC methods: GetUser (unary), ListUsers (server streaming), CreateUser (unary), and WatchUserUpdates (bidirectional streaming). Your deliverable must include: the complete .proto file with field validation rules using protoc-gen-validate, the Go server implementation with proper context deadline propagation, interceptors for request logging and Prometheus metrics, a retry policy with exponential backoff on the client side using grpc-go's built-in retry mechanism, error mapping from gRPC status codes to meaningful messages, and a Docker multi-stage build that compiles the protobuf and builds the binary in one step. Show the docker-compose entry for local development with health checks.","tags":["backend","grpc","golang","microservices","protobuf"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-33","title":"Event-Driven Order Processing with Kafka","prompt":"You are a backend engineer designing an event-driven order processing system for an e-commerce platform using Apache Kafka 3.6 and Python 3.12 with the confluent-kafka library. The system has three services: OrderService (producer), InventoryService (consumer), and NotificationService (consumer). Implement the producer and one consumer with the following requirements: the producer publishes OrderPlaced events with schema validation using Confluent Schema Registry and Avro, the consumer implements at-least-once delivery with manual offset commits after successful processing, the consumer handles rebalancing gracefully using a custom ConsumerRebalanceListener, poison pill messages are sent to a dead-letter topic after 3 failed processing attempts, and Prometheus metrics track consumer lag, processing latency, and error rate. Include the Kafka topic configuration (partitions, replication factor, retention).","tags":["backend","kafka","python","event-driven","microservices"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-34","title":"Multi-Tenant Database Schema with Row-Level Security","prompt":"You are a database architect designing the multi-tenancy strategy for a B2B SaaS platform that will serve 500 enterprise customers, each with up to 10,000 rows of sensitive financial data in PostgreSQL 15. Implement a shared database, shared schema approach using PostgreSQL Row Level Security (RLS). Your implementation must include: the tenant_id column strategy with a tenants table and FK constraints, RLS policy definitions that automatically filter every SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE to the current tenant using a session variable set at connection time, a connection pool strategy using PgBouncer in transaction mode that correctly sets the tenant context per query, an Alembic migration applying RLS to three tables (invoices, line_items, payments), performance implications (index on tenant_id for every table, explain why the planner uses it with RLS), and a security audit checklist to verify no tenant can see another's data.","tags":["backend","postgresql","multi-tenancy","security","database"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-35","title":"Redis-Backed Job Queue with Worker Pool","prompt":"You are a backend engineer building a background job processing system for a document conversion service using Node.js 20, TypeScript 5, and Redis 7. Jobs are submitted via an HTTP endpoint and processed by a pool of 5 worker processes that run ImageMagick conversions. Implement the system using the BullMQ library: define a ConversionJob interface with input S3 key, output format, and priority, configure a Queue with rate limiting (20 jobs per second), implement a Worker with concurrency of 3 per process and a custom backoff strategy (exponential, max 5 retries), add a job progress reporting hook that sends Server-Sent Events to the submitting client, implement a graceful shutdown that drains in-progress jobs before the process exits, and add a QueueEvents listener that writes job completion metrics to a Prometheus gauge. Show the complete TypeScript types for all BullMQ event payloads.","tags":["backend","redis","node.js","typescript","job-queue"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-36","title":"GraphQL API with Subscriptions and DataLoader","prompt":"You are a backend engineer building a real-time collaborative task management API with GraphQL using TypeScript, Apollo Server 4, Prisma 5, and PostgreSQL 15. The API serves a React frontend with real-time updates. Implement a complete schema covering Task, Project, and User types with: queries for paginated task lists with filtering by status and assignee, mutations for createTask, updateTask, assignTask with optimistic concurrency using a version field, a TaskUpdated subscription using Redis pub/sub for horizontal scaling across multiple Apollo Server instances, DataLoader implementations for user and project batching to eliminate N+1 queries, a custom scalar for DateTime that serializes to ISO 8601, and persisted queries using APQ to reduce bandwidth. Include the complete schema.graphql and all resolver implementations.","tags":["backend","graphql","typescript","apollo","real-time"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-37","title":"Database Migration Strategy for Zero-Downtime Deployment","prompt":"You are a senior backend engineer planning a zero-downtime database migration for a PostgreSQL 15 database with 50 million rows in a orders table that needs a new indexed column added and an existing TEXT column changed to JSONB. The service runs on Kubernetes with 3 replicas and deployments use rolling updates meaning old and new code run simultaneously for 2–5 minutes. Design the migration strategy using the expand-contract (parallel change) pattern: Phase 1 — add the nullable new column and the new JSONB column as nullable (safe to run while old code is live), Phase 2 — backfill existing rows in batches of 10,000 with a rate limit to avoid locking, Phase 3 — add NOT NULL constraint using a CHECK CONSTRAINT trick to avoid a full table scan, Phase 4 — drop the old column after new code is fully deployed. Include the Alembic migration files for each phase and explain why each step is safe for concurrent reads and writes.","tags":["backend","postgresql","database","migrations","devops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-38","title":"Idempotent Payment Webhook Handler","prompt":"You are a backend engineer building a Stripe webhook handler for a subscription billing system in Node.js 20 with TypeScript 5 and PostgreSQL 15. Stripe may deliver the same webhook event multiple times and the handler must be idempotent. Implement a production-grade webhook endpoint: validate the Stripe-Signature header using the stripe-node SDK's constructEvent method (reject invalid signatures with 400), extract the event type and handle invoice.payment_succeeded and customer.subscription.deleted, implement idempotency by storing processed event IDs in a webhook_events PostgreSQL table with a unique constraint and checking before processing, wrap the database updates and idempotency record insert in a single transaction with proper rollback handling, respond with 200 within 5 seconds to prevent Stripe retry (defer heavy processing to a BullMQ job), and add structured JSON logging for every event received, processed, and skipped as duplicate.","tags":["backend","stripe","webhooks","node.js","idempotency"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-39","title":"OpenAPI 3.1 Contract-First API Design","prompt":"You are an API architect at a platform company whose REST API is consumed by 30 external partners. You are introducing contract-first API design using OpenAPI 3.1 for a new Invoicing API. Design and document the complete OpenAPI specification for an invoicing resource with endpoints to create, retrieve, list (with filtering and pagination), and void an invoice. Your specification must include: reusable component schemas for Invoice, LineItem, Money (amount + currency code), and ErrorResponse; requestBody validation with minLength, pattern, and enum constraints; response schemas for 200, 201, 400, 401, 404, and 422 with discriminator-based error detail objects; a Webhook definition for invoice.paid events; API versioning via URL path (v1); and a security scheme using OAuth 2.0 with scopes invoices:read and invoices:write. Add x-code-samples extensions showing a curl example for each endpoint.","tags":["backend","api-design","openapi","rest","documentation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-40","title":"Connection Pool Tuning for High-Traffic API","prompt":"You are a senior backend engineer at a fintech startup whose Node.js + PostgreSQL API handles 5,000 requests per second at peak. The ops team is seeing connection_pool_timeout errors under load and database CPU spikes. The API uses pg-pool with default settings and runs on 10 Kubernetes pods. Audit the connection pool configuration and recommend optimal settings. Your analysis must cover: the formula for max pool size (max_connections in postgres ÷ number of pods, minus connections reserved for admin tools), why each pod should use a pool of 10–20 connections rather than 1 per request, the difference between pg-pool idle timeout and connection timeout, how PgBouncer in transaction mode reduces the total connections to PostgreSQL to a fixed number regardless of pod count, show the pg-pool configuration for the corrected settings, write a k6 load test that measures p99 query latency at 500 concurrent users, and explain how to read the pg_stat_activity view to confirm pool behavior.","tags":["backend","postgresql","performance","connection-pooling","node.js"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-41","title":"React 18 Virtualized Data Table","prompt":"You are a senior frontend engineer building a data table component for a financial analytics dashboard in React 18 + TypeScript 5. The table needs to render 100,000 rows of trade data with sorting, multi-column filtering, and row selection — all without pagination. Implement the component using TanStack Table v8 and TanStack Virtual v3 for row virtualization. Your implementation must include: the TypeScript generic table component with a defined ColumnDef array, virtualizer integration using useVirtualizer with a fixed row height of 48px, column header click sorting with visual asc/desc indicators, a debounced global filter input (300ms) that re-filters the full dataset, checkbox-based multi-row selection with a select-all header checkbox that handles indeterminate state, and Tailwind CSS styling with sticky headers and alternating row colors. Explain the difference between DOM virtualization and windowing and why rendering all 100,000 rows would freeze the browser.","tags":["frontend","react","typescript","performance","data-table"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:20:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"c-42","title":"Next.js App Router Data Fetching Patterns","prompt":"You are a senior Next.js 14 engineer building a product catalog page for an e-commerce storefront. You need to choose the right data fetching strategy for three sections on the same page: (1) product grid — 500 products that change every 10 minutes, (2) featured banner — content managed in a CMS, changes once per day, (3) user cart count — personalized, must be fresh on every request. Implement all three sections in the Next.js App Router using React Server Components. Show: the Server Component fetch with revalidate option for the product grid, a generateStaticParams approach for static product detail pages, the cookies() header check that forces dynamic rendering for the cart count, how to use Suspense boundaries with loading.tsx to stream each section independently, and why wrapping a personalized component in a Suspense boundary allows the rest of the page to be statically cached. Include the complete file structure under the app/ directory.","tags":["frontend","next.js","react","data-fetching","performance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-43","title":"Accessible Modal Dialog Component","prompt":"You are a frontend engineer building a reusable modal dialog component for a design system used by 8 product teams. The component must be fully accessible following WCAG 2.1 AA guidelines and WAI-ARIA authoring practices for the dialog pattern. Implement the component in React 18 + TypeScript 5 with Tailwind CSS. Your implementation must include: focus trapping inside the dialog using a custom useFocusTrap hook that cycles Tab and Shift+Tab through focusable elements, the aria-modal, role, aria-labelledby, and aria-describedby attributes correctly set, Escape key closing the dialog, focus returning to the trigger element on close, a portal rendering strategy using createPortal to avoid z-index stacking context issues, animation using CSS transitions (not JS) for open/close with prefers-reduced-motion respected, and a scroll lock on the body element. Include a usage example showing the trigger button pattern and explain why display:none is preferable to opacity:0 for hiding the dialog.","tags":["frontend","accessibility","react","typescript","design-system"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-44","title":"Zustand Global State Architecture","prompt":"You are a frontend architect designing the state management architecture for a complex React 18 SPA — a Kanban board with real-time collaboration features. The app previously used Redux Toolkit but the team wants to migrate to Zustand for simpler boilerplate. Design the complete state architecture using Zustand 4 with TypeScript 5 slices pattern. Your architecture must include: a boardSlice managing columns and cards with typed actions, a uiSlice for modal state and drag-and-drop active item, a userSlice for authentication state with persistence to localStorage via the persist middleware, a websocketSlice that subscribes to a Socket.io connection and patches board state on incoming events, the immer middleware for immutable state updates on nested card arrays, devtools integration for Redux DevTools browser extension, and a selector pattern using subscribeWithSelector to prevent unnecessary re-renders. Show how to split the store across multiple files using the slice pattern.","tags":["frontend","react","zustand","state-management","typescript"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-45","title":"CSS Grid Complex Dashboard Layout","prompt":"You are a frontend engineer building a responsive analytics dashboard layout for a SaaS product that must work across desktop (1440px), tablet (768px), and mobile (375px) without JavaScript. The desktop layout has: a fixed 240px sidebar, a top navigation bar, a main content area with a 3-column card grid, and a collapsible details panel on the right. On tablet, the sidebar collapses to icons only (64px) and the card grid becomes 2 columns. On mobile, the sidebar becomes a bottom navigation bar and cards stack to 1 column. Implement this layout using CSS Grid and CSS custom properties only — no Flexbox for the macro layout, no JavaScript. Your solution must include: the HTML structure with semantic landmark elements, named grid template areas for each breakpoint, a CSS custom property system for spacing and sidebar width, smooth sidebar collapse using CSS transitions on the custom property value, and an explanation of why grid-template-areas makes the layout intent readable.","tags":["frontend","css","layout","responsive","grid"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-46","title":"React Query Optimistic Updates","prompt":"You are a frontend engineer building a task management app in React 18 + TypeScript 5 + TanStack Query v5. Users should see instant UI feedback when they mark a task as complete, drag it to a new column, or delete it — before the server confirms the change. Implement optimistic updates for three mutations: toggleTaskComplete, moveTaskToColumn, and deleteTask. For each mutation you must: use useMutation with the onMutate, onError, and onSettled lifecycle hooks, snapshot the previous query cache state in onMutate before updating it optimistically, roll back to the snapshot in onError using the context returned from onMutate, call queryClient.invalidateQueries in onSettled to sync with the server after the mutation resolves either way, and handle the case where the server returns a conflict (409) with a toast notification. Include the full TypeScript types for each mutation context object.","tags":["frontend","react","react-query","typescript","ux"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-47","title":"Web Performance — Core Web Vitals Optimization","prompt":"You are a frontend performance engineer tasked with improving the Core Web Vitals scores of a Next.js 14 e-commerce homepage that currently scores LCP 4.2s (poor), CLS 0.18 (needs improvement), and INP 320ms (poor) in Chrome User Experience Report data. Diagnose and fix each metric. For LCP: identify the hero image as the LCP element, add fetchpriority='high' and preload link, convert to WebP with next/image responsive sizes, and eliminate render-blocking fonts with font-display: swap. For CLS: find the missing width/height on images causing layout shift, the late-loaded cookie banner that shifts content, and fix skeleton loading dimensions. For INP: profile the product filter component's click handler with Chrome DevTools Performance tab, identify the synchronous recalculation of 2,000 product items, and move it to a useTransition non-urgent update. Show before/after code for each fix with an explanation of the metric it improves.","tags":["frontend","performance","next.js","core-web-vitals","optimization"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-48","title":"Custom React Form Library with Validation","prompt":"You are a senior frontend engineer building a lightweight form management hook for a React 18 + TypeScript 5 component library. The team evaluated React Hook Form and Formik but wants a simpler internal solution with no dependencies. Implement a useForm hook from scratch that handles: typed form values with a generic <T extends Record<string, unknown>> signature, field registration with ref-based controlled inputs, synchronous and asynchronous field-level validators that return string error messages or null, form-level submission with a validateAll method that runs all validators and focuses the first invalid field, touched state tracking that only shows errors after a field has been blurred, and a FormField wrapper component that automatically connects label, input, and error message with correct aria-describedby and aria-invalid attributes. Show the hook being used to build a 5-field registration form with cross-field validation (confirm password matches password).","tags":["frontend","react","typescript","forms","hooks"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-49","title":"Micro-Frontend Module Federation Setup","prompt":"You are a frontend architect setting up a micro-frontend architecture for a large enterprise portal that currently exists as a monolithic React 18 app of 180,000 lines. Three separate product teams will own three independently deployable frontends: Shell (navigation, auth), Billing (invoices, payments), and Analytics (dashboards, reports). Set up Webpack 5 Module Federation to share React and ReactDOM as singletons, expose the BillingApp and AnalyticsApp components from their respective remotes, configure the Shell host to lazy-load remotes at runtime using dynamic imports with Suspense boundaries, show the complete webpack.config.ts for both a remote app and the host shell, explain the shared singleton requirement and what happens if React is loaded twice, handle the case where a remote is unavailable with an error boundary fallback, and provide a docker-compose.yml that starts all three apps on different ports for local development.","tags":["frontend","micro-frontend","webpack","architecture","react"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-50","title":"Real-Time Collaborative Text Editor","prompt":"You are a frontend engineer building a real-time collaborative text editor feature for a documentation product, similar to Google Docs. The editor is built with React 18 + TypeScript 5 and needs to support concurrent editing by up to 20 users with conflict resolution. Implement the operational transformation or CRDT approach using the Yjs library with the y-websocket provider. Your implementation must include: a Yjs Y.Text shared type bound to a contenteditable div using a custom React hook, a WebSocket provider connecting to a y-websocket server for real-time sync, a user awareness protocol showing colored cursors and user names for each connected collaborator, undo/redo that is scoped to the local user's changes (not undoing other users' edits), persistence of the Yjs document state to IndexedDB so the document loads offline, and an explanation of how CRDTs guarantee convergence to the same state without a central arbiter, contrasting with operational transformation.","tags":["frontend","react","crdt","real-time","collaboration"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-51","title":"TDD Cycle for a Payment Validation Service","prompt":"You are a senior engineer pair-programming with a junior using strict Test-Driven Development on a payment validation service in TypeScript 5 with Vitest. The service must validate credit card numbers using the Luhn algorithm, verify expiry dates are not in the past, and check CVV format by card type (Amex: 4 digits, others: 3 digits). Drive the implementation using the red-green-refactor cycle. Write the failing tests first for all cases: valid Luhn number passes, invalid Luhn fails, expired card fails, future expiry passes, Amex CVV with 4 digits passes, Amex CVV with 3 digits fails, Visa CVV with 3 digits passes, Visa CVV with 4 digits fails, empty inputs throw a ValidationError. After all tests are written and failing, implement the minimum code to make each test pass. After all tests pass, refactor for clean code. Show each phase (red, green, refactor) separately with the test file and implementation file side by side.","tags":["testing","tdd","typescript","vitest","clean-code"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:10:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"c-52","title":"Jest Unit Tests for a Redux Toolkit Slice","prompt":"You are a frontend engineer writing comprehensive unit tests for a Redux Toolkit slice that manages shopping cart state in a React 18 e-commerce app. The slice has reducers for addItem, removeItem, updateQuantity, clearCart, and an async thunk applyDiscountCode that calls a pricing API. Write a complete Vitest test suite covering: each reducer with the full state shape verified (not just changed fields), the addItem reducer handling the case where the item already exists (quantity increment), the updateQuantity reducer with a quantity of 0 triggering item removal, the applyDiscountCode thunk with msw (Mock Service Worker) intercepting the API call for the success case (200 with discount amount), the pending/rejected states for the thunk with a mocked 422 response, and two selector tests using reselect's createSelector for cartItemCount and cartTotalAfterDiscount. Target 100% branch coverage for the slice file.","tags":["testing","redux","typescript","vitest","frontend"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-53","title":"Pytest Integration Tests for FastAPI + PostgreSQL","prompt":"You are a backend engineer writing integration tests for a FastAPI 0.111 user registration and authentication API backed by PostgreSQL 15. The tests must run against a real PostgreSQL instance (not mocked) to catch ORM and migration issues. Set up the test infrastructure using pytest 8, httpx AsyncClient, and a test database that is created fresh for each test session. Your test suite must include: a conftest.py with a session-scoped async PostgreSQL database fixture using asyncpg that runs all Alembic migrations, a function-scoped fixture that rolls back each test in a transaction (not dropping/recreating tables), tests for POST /auth/register covering duplicate email 409, weak password 422, and success 201 with the response body shape verified, tests for POST /auth/login covering wrong password 401 and success with a JWT in the response, an authenticated test using a pytest fixture that registers and logs in a user and yields the access token, and a test for GET /users/me that verifies the token decodes to the correct user. Show the full conftest.py and one test file.","tags":["testing","pytest","fastapi","postgresql","integration"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-54","title":"Cypress E2E Test — Checkout Flow","prompt":"You are a QA engineer writing Cypress 13 end-to-end tests for the critical checkout flow of a Next.js 14 e-commerce app. The flow spans five steps: product selection, cart review, shipping address, payment (Stripe Elements), and order confirmation. Write a complete Cypress test suite that: uses cy.intercept to stub the Stripe payment intent API so tests don't make real charges, seeds the test database before each test using a cy.task command that calls a seed script, uses Page Object Model pattern with a separate class per page step, tests the happy path from product page to order confirmation email trigger, tests form validation errors on the shipping address step (missing required fields, invalid postal code format), tests that an out-of-stock product cannot be added to cart (add to cart button disabled, correct aria-disabled attribute), and captures a Percy visual snapshot on the order confirmation page for regression detection. Include the cypress.config.ts with the task definitions.","tags":["testing","cypress","e2e","next.js","frontend"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-55","title":"Property-Based Testing with Hypothesis","prompt":"You are a Python engineer writing property-based tests for a financial calculation library using Hypothesis 6 with pytest. The library handles currency conversion, compound interest calculations, and portfolio rebalancing. Explain why example-based tests miss edge cases for numeric code, then implement property-based test strategies for: a currency conversion function (property: converting A→B then B→A returns the original amount within floating-point epsilon), a compound interest function (property: more frequent compounding periods always yield equal or higher returns), a portfolio normalization function (property: weights always sum to 1.0 regardless of input allocation values, and no weight is ever negative), a date-range generator (property: the output list is always sorted and contains no duplicates). For each test, show the @given decorator strategy, the property assertion, and one example of a counterexample Hypothesis found during development.","tags":["testing","python","hypothesis","property-based","finance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-56","title":"Contract Testing with Pact for Microservices","prompt":"You are a backend engineer introducing consumer-driven contract testing between a React frontend (consumer) and a Node.js order API (provider) using Pact JS 12. The team has been burned by integration failures discovered only in staging when the API changed a response field name. Set up a complete Pact workflow: on the consumer side, write a Pact test using PactV3 that defines the expected interaction for GET /orders/{id} including the request matcher and response body shape with Pact matchers (like, eachLike, regex), publish the generated pact file to a PactBroker instance, on the provider side, write a Pact verification test using the Pact Node provider that replays the consumer contract against the real Express router with a beforeEach state handler, configure the PactBroker webhook to block the provider's CI pipeline if it breaks a consumer contract, and show the GitHub Actions workflow that runs provider verification on every PR with a can-i-deploy check before merging.","tags":["testing","pact","contract-testing","microservices","ci-cd"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-57","title":"Go Table-Driven Tests with Subtests","prompt":"You are a Go engineer writing idiomatic tests for an HTTP middleware package that handles request authentication, rate limiting, and request ID injection for a Go 1.22 API server. Write a comprehensive test suite using Go's built-in testing package. Your test suite must demonstrate: table-driven tests for the authentication middleware covering valid JWT, expired JWT, malformed token, missing Authorization header, and Bearer prefix without token — each as a separate entry in a []struct test table, subtests using t.Run for each table entry so they can be run individually with -run, a httptest.NewRecorder and httptest.NewRequest for each test without needing a running server, a test helper function setupTestMiddleware(t *testing.T) that creates the middleware chain with a mock JWT validator, parallel tests using t.Parallel() with an explanation of when this is and isn't safe, and benchmark tests for the rate limiter middleware at 10,000 requests to measure allocations per operation using b.ReportAllocs().","tags":["testing","golang","unit-tests","middleware","benchmarking"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-58","title":"k6 Load Test for an API at Scale","prompt":"You are a performance engineer designing a load test for a new REST API endpoint POST /api/v1/checkout that processes orders and is expected to handle 500 requests per second at peak Black Friday traffic. Write a complete k6 v0.50 load test script that: defines four test stages using the stages option (ramp up to 100 VUs over 1 minute, sustain 500 VUs for 5 minutes, spike to 1000 VUs for 1 minute, ramp down), sets custom thresholds (http_req_duration p95 must be under 300ms, error rate must be under 1%), parameterizes test data by reading a CSV file of test users so VUs use different credentials, groups related requests using k6 groups and checks for status code 201 and the presence of an orderId in the response body, adds custom metrics using Counter and Trend for tracking checkout abandonment and payment processing time specifically, and exports metrics to a Prometheus remote write endpoint for Grafana dashboards. Explain the difference between VUs and requests per second and how to calculate the VU count needed for a target RPS.","tags":["testing","performance","k6","load-testing","devops"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-59","title":"Snapshot and Visual Regression Testing in Storybook","prompt":"You are a frontend engineer setting up visual regression testing for a React 18 component library of 60 components. The current approach of manually reviewing component screenshots in PRs is not scaling. Set up an automated visual testing pipeline using Storybook 8 and Chromatic. Your setup must include: a Button component story with Controls for variant (primary, secondary, destructive), size (sm, md, lg), disabled state, and loading state — covering all 18 combinations with play functions testing keyboard navigation, configuration of Chromatic with a project token in GitHub Actions that captures a snapshot of every story on every PR, a turbosnap configuration that only re-tests stories whose component source files changed, a baseline acceptance workflow where the team lead must approve visual diffs in the Chromatic UI before a PR can merge, interaction tests using @storybook/test that assert the onClick is called and the loading spinner appears, and an explanation of the difference between snapshot tests (DOM structure) and visual regression tests (pixel diff).","tags":["testing","storybook","visual-regression","react","ci-cd"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-60","title":"Code Coverage Strategy for a Legacy Codebase","prompt":"You are a tech lead creating a testing strategy for a 5-year-old Python 3.12 Django monolith with 12% test coverage. The codebase processes medical device telemetry data, so untested code paths represent patient safety risk. Design a pragmatic coverage improvement plan that the team of 4 can execute over 3 sprints. Your plan must include: how to generate a coverage report with pytest-cov and identify the 20% of modules that handle 80% of production traffic using Django request logs, a policy of requiring 80% coverage on any new code merged (enforced in CI with --fail-under=80 on changed files only using diff-cover), a strangler fig approach for adding tests to existing modules without refactoring them first (characterization tests that lock existing behavior), a prioritized list of the 5 module types that should be tested first (payment processing, auth, data validation, API serializers, background tasks), how to mock Django's ORM calls correctly using pytest-django's @pytest.mark.django_db and freezegun for time-dependent tests, and metrics to track on a weekly dashboard (coverage %, tests added, mutation score using mutmut).","tags":["testing","python","django","code-coverage","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-61","title":"GitHub Actions CI/CD Pipeline for a Monorepo","prompt":"You are a DevOps engineer building a CI/CD pipeline for a monorepo containing a Next.js frontend, a FastAPI Python backend, and a shared TypeScript library. The pipeline must be fast and only test and deploy services that have changed. Design the complete GitHub Actions workflow using: path filters in the on.push trigger to detect changes per service directory, a matrix strategy that runs linting, type checking, and tests for each changed service in parallel, Docker build and push to Amazon ECR using OIDC authentication (not stored AWS credentials), a Kubernetes rolling deployment using kubectl with a post-deploy health check that polls the /health endpoint for 60 seconds and rolls back automatically on failure, environment-specific jobs (staging deploys on push to main, production deploys on a manual workflow_dispatch with a required approval), and caching for pip, npm, and Docker layer caches to bring the total pipeline time under 5 minutes. Include the full .github/workflows/ci.yml file.","tags":["devops","github-actions","ci-cd","kubernetes","monorepo"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:00:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"c-62","title":"Dockerfile Multi-Stage Build Optimization","prompt":"You are a DevOps engineer optimizing Docker images for a Node.js 20 TypeScript application that currently builds a 1.8 GB production image and takes 4 minutes to rebuild after any code change. The application is a Next.js 14 SSR app with 200 npm dependencies. Rewrite the Dockerfile using multi-stage builds to solve both problems. Your Dockerfile must include: a deps stage that installs only production dependencies using npm ci --only=production, a builder stage that installs all dependencies and runs next build, a runner stage using node:20-alpine that copies only the .next/standalone output and public assets (not node_modules), a non-root user created with addgroup and adduser for security, COPY instructions ordered from least to most frequently changing files to maximize layer cache hits, a .dockerignore file that excludes .git, node_modules, .next, and test files, and a Makefile target that shows the commands to build, tag, and push to ECR. Show the image size comparison before and after with docker images output.","tags":["devops","docker","optimization","node.js","ci-cd"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-63","title":"Terraform AWS Infrastructure for a SaaS Platform","prompt":"You are a cloud engineer writing Terraform 1.8 infrastructure-as-code for a new SaaS product launching on AWS. The infrastructure needs: a VPC with public and private subnets across 3 availability zones, an EKS 1.29 cluster in private subnets with managed node groups, an RDS PostgreSQL 15 Multi-AZ instance in private subnets, an ElastiCache Redis 7 cluster, an Application Load Balancer with ACM SSL certificates, and an S3 bucket with CloudFront CDN for static assets. Structure the code using reusable Terraform modules (vpc, eks, rds, redis, cdn), use remote state in S3 with DynamoDB locking, define variable files for staging and production environments, add lifecycle rules to prevent accidental RDS deletion, and use data sources to look up the latest EKS-optimized AMI. Show the module call graph and explain the dependency ordering that prevents a destroy-and-recreate cycle on the EKS node groups.","tags":["devops","terraform","aws","infrastructure","iac"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-64","title":"Prometheus + Grafana Observability Stack","prompt":"You are an SRE setting up the observability stack for a FastAPI microservice running on Kubernetes 1.29. The service handles document processing and the team needs dashboards for the four golden signals: latency, traffic, errors, and saturation. Implement the complete observability setup: instrument the FastAPI app with prometheus-fastapi-instrumentator to expose /metrics with request duration histograms, error rate counters, and in-flight request gauges, add custom business metrics using the Prometheus Python client (documents_processed_total, processing_duration_seconds as a Histogram with custom buckets), deploy Prometheus using the kube-prometheus-stack Helm chart with a ServiceMonitor CRD that scrapes the FastAPI pods, create a Grafana dashboard JSON with four panels (p50/p95/p99 latency by endpoint, requests per second, 5xx error rate, and CPU saturation), write three Prometheus alerting rules (p99 latency > 2s for 5 minutes, error rate > 5% for 2 minutes, pod CPU > 80% for 10 minutes), and configure PagerDuty as the alert receiver in the Alertmanager config.","tags":["devops","prometheus","grafana","kubernetes","observability"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-65","title":"Helm Chart for a Microservice Deployment","prompt":"You are a DevOps engineer packaging a Go microservice for deployment to multiple Kubernetes environments using Helm 3. The service runs as a Deployment with a horizontal pod autoscaler, needs a ConfigMap and a Secret for configuration, exposes an HTTP port via a Service and Ingress, and has different resource limits for staging versus production. Create a complete Helm chart with: a Chart.yaml with semantic versioning, a values.yaml with sensible defaults for image tag, replica count, resource requests/limits, and ingress hostname, a deployment.yaml template using helper functions from _helpers.tpl for labels and the fullname, a hpa.yaml template that enables only if autoscaling.enabled is true with configurable minReplicas and maxReplicas, a secret.yaml that base64-encodes values from values.yaml and mounts them as environment variables, an ingress.yaml supporting both nginx and AWS ALB ingress class via a values switch, and a values-production.yaml override file. Show the helm upgrade --install command and how to diff a pending release with helm diff.","tags":["devops","kubernetes","helm","deployment","infrastructure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-66","title":"Database Backup and Point-in-Time Recovery","prompt":"You are a database reliability engineer designing the backup and recovery strategy for a PostgreSQL 15 database storing financial transaction records for a fintech startup. The RPO (Recovery Point Objective) is 1 hour and the RTO (Recovery Time Objective) is 2 hours. Design and implement the complete backup strategy using AWS: configure continuous WAL archiving to S3 using archive_command in postgresql.conf so every WAL segment is uploaded, set up daily base backups using pg_basebackup with --checkpoint=fast and --wal-method=stream, write a shell script that takes a base backup, uploads it to S3 with server-side encryption, and verifies the backup is restorable by doing a trial restore to a test instance weekly, implement point-in-time recovery to a specific timestamp by replaying WAL segments from S3 using recovery_target_time in recovery.conf, set up a Prometheus alert if no WAL file has been uploaded to S3 in the past 15 minutes, and document the runbook steps an on-call engineer follows to recover from a dropped table.","tags":["devops","postgresql","backup","disaster-recovery","aws"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-67","title":"Blue-Green Deployment with Feature Flags","prompt":"You are a platform engineer implementing a blue-green deployment strategy for a critical payment processing service running on AWS ECS Fargate. The service processes $2M in transactions daily and cannot tolerate downtime. Design the deployment system combining blue-green with feature flags using AWS CodeDeploy and LaunchDarkly. Your design must include: the ECS service configuration with two target groups (blue and green) behind an ALB, a CodeDeploy AppSpec.yml that routes 10% of traffic to green for 5 minutes, then 50% for 5 minutes, then 100% with automatic rollback if the ECS health check fails, a pre-traffic Lambda hook that runs smoke tests against the green environment before traffic shifts, a LaunchDarkly feature flag in the application code that controls the new payment processing algorithm — allowing it to be toggled off in 30 seconds without a deployment, canary metrics in CloudWatch that trigger the CodeDeploy rollback if p99 latency exceeds 500ms, and a runbook for the on-call engineer showing how to manually trigger a rollback.","tags":["devops","blue-green","deployment","aws","feature-flags"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-68","title":"Linux Performance Diagnosis Runbook","prompt":"You are a senior SRE writing a performance diagnosis runbook for a team of mid-level engineers who are on-call for a set of Linux Ubuntu 22.04 application servers. A server is showing high load average but the application team says CPU usage looks normal in their dashboards. Write a step-by-step diagnosis runbook covering: using uptime and top to distinguish CPU-bound vs I/O-bound load (the difference between load average and CPU utilization %), using iostat -xz 1 to identify which disk is saturated and what processes are causing it, using vmstat 1 to diagnose memory pressure and swap usage leading to page faults, using ss -s and netstat to identify connection state accumulation (TIME_WAIT flood, SYN backlog), using strace -p PID to diagnose a stuck process making unexpected system calls, using perf top to identify a CPU hot path in application code, and a decision tree diagram in ASCII art showing which tool to run based on the initial symptom. Format as a Confluence-ready runbook with command examples and expected output explanations.","tags":["devops","linux","performance","sre","observability"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-69","title":"Secrets Management with HashiCorp Vault","prompt":"You are a security engineer replacing hardcoded environment variables in a Kubernetes-deployed Node.js application with a proper secrets management solution using HashiCorp Vault 1.16. The app currently stores database passwords, API keys, and TLS certificates in Kubernetes Secrets (base64 encoded, not encrypted at rest). Implement the Vault integration: enable the Kubernetes auth method so pods can authenticate to Vault using their service account token without storing any Vault token in the pod, create a Vault policy that grants the app's service account read access only to the specific secret paths it needs, use the Vault Agent Injector as a Kubernetes mutating webhook to inject secrets as environment variables or files into pods via annotations, configure dynamic database credentials using Vault's PostgreSQL secrets engine so the app gets short-lived credentials (TTL of 1 hour) that are automatically rotated, set up Vault audit logging to CloudWatch, and write a comparison explaining why Vault dynamic secrets are more secure than static secrets even if both are stored encrypted.","tags":["devops","security","vault","kubernetes","secrets"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-70","title":"OpenTelemetry Distributed Tracing","prompt":"You are an SRE instrumenting a distributed system of 4 microservices (API Gateway, Order Service, Inventory Service, Notification Service) with distributed tracing using OpenTelemetry 1.24 and Jaeger. The team currently has no tracing and cannot tell which service is adding latency to a slow checkout request that spans all 4 services. Implement end-to-end tracing: instrument the Node.js API Gateway with the @opentelemetry/sdk-node auto-instrumentation package that patches HTTP, gRPC, and PostgreSQL calls automatically, add manual span creation in the Python Order Service for the business-critical reserve_inventory step with custom attributes (order_id, item_count, total_value), configure W3C TraceContext propagation headers so the trace ID flows across HTTP calls between services, deploy the OpenTelemetry Collector as a DaemonSet that receives OTLP from all services, batches spans, and exports to Jaeger, create a Jaeger query showing the critical path for a full checkout trace with timing per service, and calculate the p99 end-to-end checkout latency from the trace data using a Jaeger Prometheus metrics query.","tags":["devops","opentelemetry","tracing","observability","microservices"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-71","title":"OWASP Top 10 Security Audit","prompt":"You are a security engineer conducting an application security audit of a Node.js 20 + Express.js REST API before a SOC 2 Type II audit. Review the code below against the OWASP Top 10 2021. For each vulnerability found, provide: the OWASP category and number, the exact line in the code where it appears, a proof-of-concept attack payload showing how an attacker would exploit it, the corrected code with an explanation of why the fix prevents the attack, and the npm package or built-in Node.js feature used to implement the fix. Pay particular attention to: A01 Broken Access Control (missing authorization checks on admin routes), A02 Cryptographic Failures (MD5 password hashing, HTTP not HTTPS), A03 Injection (concatenated SQL strings), and A05 Security Misconfiguration (default Express headers exposing X-Powered-By).\n\n[Paste your code here]","tags":["security","owasp","node.js","code-review","audit"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:50:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"c-72","title":"JWT vs Session Auth — Implementation Comparison","prompt":"You are a senior security engineer advising a team building authentication for a React SPA + Node.js API that will also have a mobile app consumer. The team is debating between stateless JWT authentication and stateful server-side sessions. Produce a detailed comparison covering both approaches. For JWT: implement RS256 token generation using the jose library with 15-minute access tokens and 7-day refresh tokens stored in httpOnly cookies, show the refresh token rotation pattern where each use of a refresh token issues a new one and invalidates the old, implement a token blacklist using Redis to enable logout before expiry. For sessions: implement express-session with Redis store, show the session fixation attack and the fix (regenerate session ID on login). Conclude with a recommendation for this specific use case (SPA + mobile) and explain why storing JWTs in localStorage is dangerous compared to httpOnly cookies, including a concrete XSS attack demonstration.","tags":["security","jwt","authentication","node.js","sessions"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-73","title":"SQL Injection Prevention — Parameterized Queries","prompt":"You are a security engineer reviewing a Python 3.12 + SQLAlchemy application that was flagged in a penetration test for SQL injection vulnerabilities. The pen tester provided three payloads that successfully extracted data from the database and bypassed authentication. Analyze the vulnerable code below. For each injection point: demonstrate the exact SQL query generated by the injection payload (show the malicious SQL string), explain why string concatenation and f-strings for SQL are never safe even with manual escaping, show the corrected parameterized query using SQLAlchemy's text() with bindparams or the ORM query API, explain the difference between parameterized queries (safe) and prepared statements (also safe but different mechanism), and add a SQLMap detection note explaining what tool the pen tester likely used. Finally, implement an input validation layer using Pydantic v2 that prevents the injection payloads from even reaching the database layer.\n\n[Paste the SQL query or schema]","tags":["security","sql-injection","python","sqlalchemy","owasp"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-74","title":"OAuth 2.0 + PKCE Implementation for SPA","prompt":"You are a security engineer implementing OAuth 2.0 with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) for a React 18 SPA that authenticates users via an external identity provider (Auth0). The previous implicit flow implementation stored access tokens in localStorage and is being replaced. Implement the complete PKCE flow: generate a cryptographically random code_verifier using window.crypto.getRandomValues, derive the code_challenge using SHA-256 and base64url encoding, initiate the authorization request with the challenge and store the verifier in sessionStorage (not localStorage), handle the callback route by exchanging the code + verifier for tokens, store access tokens only in memory (a React context or Zustand store) and refresh tokens in httpOnly cookies via a thin backend endpoint, implement a silent refresh using a hidden iframe or refresh token rotation before the access token expires, and write a security analysis explaining why PKCE prevents authorization code interception attacks and why the implicit flow was deprecated in OAuth 2.1.","tags":["security","oauth","pkce","react","authentication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-75","title":"Content Security Policy Configuration","prompt":"You are a security engineer hardening a Next.js 14 application against XSS attacks by implementing a strict Content Security Policy. The app uses Google Analytics, Stripe.js for payments, self-hosted fonts, and inline styles generated by a CSS-in-JS library. Implement a production-grade CSP: explain why a permissive default-src * policy is equivalent to having no CSP, generate a strict CSP header in next.config.ts using the headers() API with a nonce-based approach for inline scripts (since unsafe-inline breaks strict CSP), implement the nonce generation middleware that creates a new crypto nonce per request and injects it into the CSP header and the HTML script tags using Next.js's generateNonces approach, configure the script-src to allow Google Analytics and Stripe.js domains explicitly, add report-uri pointing to a CSP violation collection endpoint, test the policy with Google CSP Evaluator, and show the correct CSP for each Stripe.js requirement (Stripe requires frame-src for the payment iframe).","tags":["security","csp","next.js","xss","headers"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-76","title":"Rate Limiting and Brute Force Protection","prompt":"You are a security engineer hardening the authentication endpoints of a Node.js 20 + Express.js API against brute force attacks. The current API has no rate limiting and a security audit found that an attacker can try 10,000 password combinations per minute against any account. Implement a layered defense: deploy express-rate-limit on all authentication endpoints (5 requests per 15 minutes per IP for POST /auth/login), implement account lockout after 10 failed attempts using Redis to track per-account failure counts with a 30-minute TTL, add CAPTCHA challenge (reCAPTCHA v3 with a 0.5 score threshold) after 3 consecutive failures from the same IP, implement timing-attack-safe password comparison using bcrypt.compare (constant time) and explain why comparing password hashes with === leaks timing information, log all authentication failures to a SIEM with IP, user agent, and timestamp, and set up a CloudWatch alarm that pages on-call when login failure rate exceeds 100 per minute across all IPs (credential stuffing pattern).","tags":["security","rate-limiting","authentication","node.js","brute-force"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-77","title":"Dependency Vulnerability Management","prompt":"You are a security engineer establishing a software supply chain security program for a team of 12 engineers across 5 Node.js and Python microservices. The company had no process for tracking CVEs in dependencies and was recently notified of a critical vulnerability in a transitive dependency. Design and implement the vulnerability management program: set up Dependabot on GitHub for automated PRs on critical and high severity CVEs with a 48-hour SLA, configure npm audit and pip-audit in the CI pipeline to fail builds on critical vulnerabilities using --audit-level=critical, implement SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) generation using syft in the Docker build pipeline and store it as a build artifact in GitHub, use Trivy container scanning in the GitHub Actions workflow to scan the final Docker image and block deploys if CRITICAL CVEs are found, create a vulnerability triage runbook covering: CVSS score assessment, exploitability context, patch availability, and temporary mitigation steps, and set up a Slack notification when a new CVE is published for any package in the SBOM.","tags":["security","dependencies","supply-chain","devops","ci-cd"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-78","title":"Secrets Scanning and Rotation","prompt":"You are a security engineer responding to an incident where a developer accidentally committed an AWS access key to a public GitHub repository 3 hours ago. GitHub Secret Scanning detected it and sent an alert. Walk through the incident response and prevention strategy. Immediate response: revoke the AWS access key using the AWS CLI within the next 5 minutes, audit CloudTrail for any API calls made with the key in the past 3 hours, check if the key was used by any unauthorized IP or for any unusual service calls. Git history cleanup: use git filter-repo (not BFG) to remove the commit from history and force-push, explain why force-pushing does not guarantee removal from GitHub caches. Prevention: set up git-secrets pre-commit hook to block commits containing AWS key patterns, configure GitHub Advanced Security secret scanning with push protection to block pushes containing secrets at the server side, migrate the application to use IAM roles for EC2/EKS instead of access keys, and write a post-incident review template.","tags":["security","secrets","incident-response","aws","git"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-79","title":"Encryption at Rest and in Transit","prompt":"You are a security engineer designing the data encryption strategy for a healthcare SaaS platform that stores PHI (Protected Health Information) and must comply with HIPAA technical safeguards. Design the complete encryption architecture covering both at-rest and in-transit requirements. At rest: configure PostgreSQL 15 on AWS RDS with AWS KMS encryption using a customer-managed key (CMK) with automatic annual rotation, implement field-level encryption for Social Security Numbers and medical record numbers using Python's cryptography library with AES-256-GCM, explain why field-level encryption protects against a database backup theft even if disk encryption is compromised. In transit: enforce TLS 1.3 minimum on all service-to-service communication by configuring the appropriate ssl_min_protocol_version in nginx and PostgreSQL, implement mutual TLS (mTLS) between microservices using cert-manager on Kubernetes with a self-hosted CA, and write the AWS Config rule that alerts when any RDS instance is created without encryption enabled.","tags":["security","encryption","hipaa","aws","cryptography"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-80","title":"Role-Based Access Control Implementation","prompt":"You are a backend engineer implementing a fine-grained Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) system for a multi-tenant project management SaaS built with FastAPI 0.111 and PostgreSQL 15. The system has three roles per project: Owner (full control), Editor (read/write), and Viewer (read only), and permissions must be checked at the resource level, not just the route level. Design and implement the RBAC system: define the database schema (users, roles, permissions, user_project_roles tables) with proper indices, implement a FastAPI dependency get_current_user_project_permission(required_permission) that checks the JWT's user ID against the user_project_roles table for the resource being accessed, create a Permission enum with values CREATE, READ, UPDATE, DELETE, MANAGE_MEMBERS and a role-to-permissions mapping, add a project-level permission check decorator that can be applied to any endpoint, write the SQL query that fetches a user's effective permissions for a project in a single round trip using a JOIN, and test the authorization boundaries with pytest ensuring a Viewer cannot call the PATCH /projects/{id} endpoint even with a valid JWT.","tags":["security","rbac","fastapi","authorization","python"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-81","title":"Microservices vs Monolith Decision Framework","prompt":"You are a principal engineer advising a 12-person engineering team at a Series A startup that currently has a monolithic Django application serving 50,000 users. The CTO wants to migrate to microservices because competitors are doing it, but the team is struggling with feature velocity. Produce a structured architectural decision report that covers: the actual problems with the current monolith (slow test suite, long deploy cycles, tight coupling between modules) versus the problems that microservices solve (independent scaling, independent deployability), Martin Fowler's monolith-first principle and why premature decomposition is expensive, a strangler fig migration plan for extracting two high-value services (authentication and notifications) without a big-bang rewrite, the operational costs of microservices (service discovery, distributed tracing, eventual consistency) that the team is not yet equipped for, a modular monolith as a middle-ground architecture using Django apps with enforced inter-module contracts, and a concrete decision matrix scoring the two options against the team's current constraints.","tags":["architecture","microservices","system-design","decision-making","backend"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:40:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"c-82","title":"Event Sourcing Architecture for Audit Trail","prompt":"You are a software architect designing an event sourcing system for a financial trading platform where every state change must be auditable and replayable for regulatory compliance. The system processes 10,000 trade events per second and must maintain a complete history of every order's state transitions. Design the complete event sourcing architecture using Python 3.12, Apache Kafka 3.6, and PostgreSQL 15 as the event store. Your design must cover: the Event base class with event_id, aggregate_id, event_type, payload, and timestamp fields, the Order aggregate that applies events to reconstruct current state (OrderPlaced, OrderFilled, OrderCancelled), an event store implementation that appends events with optimistic concurrency control (expected_version check to prevent lost updates), a Kafka event bus that publishes events to derived read models (a PostgreSQL projection table for order status queries), snapshotting strategy for aggregates with more than 100 events to avoid full replay on load, and a complete replay scenario showing how to rebuild a corrupted read model from the event store.","tags":["architecture","event-sourcing","kafka","python","system-design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-83","title":"CQRS Pattern for a High-Read API","prompt":"You are a backend architect implementing the CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) pattern for an e-commerce product catalog that has 1,000 writes per minute but 100,000 reads per minute. The current single PostgreSQL database is becoming a bottleneck because complex read queries with joins are locking rows during write operations. Design the CQRS implementation using TypeScript, Node.js 20, PostgreSQL 15 as the write store, and Elasticsearch 8 as the read store. Your design must include: the Command side — ProductCreatedCommand, UpdatePriceCommand handlers that write to PostgreSQL and publish domain events to a Redis stream, the Query side — a product search query handler that reads from Elasticsearch with full-text search, faceted filtering, and sorting without touching PostgreSQL, an event handler that listens to the Redis stream and synchronizes Elasticsearch with a 500ms eventual consistency window, the tradeoffs of eventual consistency for search (stale search results for up to 500ms after a price update), and a comparison of when CQRS adds complexity without benefit versus when it genuinely solves a scaling problem.","tags":["architecture","cqrs","system-design","elasticsearch","typescript"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-84","title":"API Gateway Pattern Design","prompt":"You are a solutions architect designing an API Gateway for a microservices platform that has 15 backend services and is consumed by a React web app, a React Native mobile app, and third-party partners via API keys. The backend services use a mix of REST and gRPC. Design the API Gateway using Kong Gateway 3.6 with Kubernetes ingress. Your design must cover: request routing and protocol translation (gRPC to REST transcoding for mobile clients), authentication plugins — OAuth 2.0 + PKCE for web, API key authentication for partners, JWT verification for internal service-to-service calls, rate limiting per consumer tier (partner free: 100/min, partner paid: 10,000/min, internal: unlimited), request and response transformation (camelCase to snake_case field mapping for a legacy service), circuit breaker configuration using the proxy-cache plugin with Redis for idempotent GET requests, plugin execution order and how Kong's priority system works, and a comparison with building a custom Node.js API gateway versus using Kong.","tags":["architecture","api-gateway","kong","microservices","system-design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-85","title":"Saga Pattern for Distributed Transactions","prompt":"You are a backend architect solving the distributed transaction problem for an e-commerce checkout flow that spans three microservices: OrderService (creates the order record), InventoryService (reserves stock), and PaymentService (charges the card). These services have separate PostgreSQL databases and cannot participate in a 2-phase commit. Design the Saga pattern implementation using choreography-based sagas with Apache Kafka 3.6. Your design must include: the forward transaction steps and the compensating transactions for each failure scenario (OrderService creates order → InventoryService reserves stock → PaymentService charges card), the Kafka topics and event types for each step and its compensating event, idempotent event consumers for each service that use a processed_events table to handle duplicate delivery, the failure scenario where PaymentService fails after InventoryService has reserved stock — trace the compensating events that release the reservation and cancel the order, a state machine diagram for the Order aggregate's valid state transitions, and a comparison of choreography vs orchestration sagas explaining when to use a Saga orchestrator (like Temporal.io) instead.","tags":["architecture","saga","distributed-systems","kafka","microservices"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-86","title":"Database Sharding Strategy","prompt":"You are a database architect designing the sharding strategy for a multi-tenant analytics platform where the users table has grown to 500 million rows and single-node PostgreSQL 15 can no longer handle the write throughput of 50,000 inserts per second. Design the horizontal sharding architecture. Your design must cover: shard key selection analysis (tenant_id as the shard key for data locality vs user_id for write distribution — evaluate tradeoffs), consistent hashing for shard assignment with 1,024 virtual shards mapped to 8 physical Citus nodes, the application-level shard routing layer in Python 3.12 that computes the target node from the shard key without a central metadata server, cross-shard query handling for administrative reports that aggregate across all tenants using Citus distributed tables and reference tables, the re-sharding process for adding a 9th node without downtime using Citus's shard rebalancer, and hot shard detection — monitoring for shards that receive disproportionate traffic and strategies for splitting them.","tags":["architecture","database","sharding","postgresql","system-design"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-87","title":"Hexagonal Architecture Implementation","prompt":"You are a senior software engineer refactoring a Node.js 20 + TypeScript 5 monolith to use Hexagonal Architecture (Ports and Adapters) to make it testable and framework-independent. The current codebase has Express.js routes directly calling Prisma ORM queries and sending Mailgun emails — the business logic is inseparable from infrastructure. Refactor one domain module (User Registration) using Hexagonal Architecture. Your implementation must include: the domain model User as a plain TypeScript class with no framework dependencies, a UserRepository port as a TypeScript interface, a PostgreSQL adapter implementing the UserRepository interface using Prisma 5, an EmailNotificationPort interface and a Mailgun adapter implementing it, the RegisterUserUseCase application service that depends only on the ports (interfaces), an Express.js HTTP adapter that translates HTTP requests to use case calls, a unit-testable RegisterUserUseCase test using in-memory adapters instead of real database and email, and a directory structure diagram showing the separation of domain, application, and infrastructure layers.","tags":["architecture","hexagonal","typescript","clean-architecture","node.js"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-88","title":"Caching Strategy — Cache-Aside vs Write-Through","prompt":"You are a backend architect designing the caching strategy for a product catalog API that serves 500,000 requests per minute with a cache hit rate target of 95%. The current system has no caching and every request hits PostgreSQL 15. Design a multi-layer caching architecture using Redis 7 and CDN. Compare and implement three caching patterns for different data characteristics: Cache-Aside (lazy loading) for product detail pages — show the get from cache → cache miss → fetch from DB → populate cache flow with a Redis TTL of 10 minutes and a Python implementation using aioredis, Write-Through for product inventory counts — show how every inventory update writes to both PostgreSQL and Redis atomically using a Lua script to prevent cache inconsistency, Read-Through for category trees — cached for 1 hour with a background refresh job that pre-warms the cache before expiry, and CDN edge caching for product images using Cache-Control headers with stale-while-revalidate. For each pattern, state when it introduces cache inconsistency and how to handle stale data.","tags":["architecture","caching","redis","system-design","performance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-89","title":"Observability-Driven Architecture Design","prompt":"You are a principal engineer designing observability into a new checkout microservice from day one, before writing the first line of business logic. The service is built with Go 1.22 and will process 200 transactions per second. Design the observability architecture as a first-class architectural concern using the three pillars. Metrics: define the RED metrics (Request rate, Error rate, Duration) as Prometheus histograms and counters, add business metrics for payment_authorization_rate and cart_abandonment_rate as gauges, implement exemplars that link a high-latency metric data point directly to its trace ID. Tracing: use OpenTelemetry Go SDK to instrument every database call, Redis operation, and external API call with spans, propagate trace context across service boundaries using W3C TraceContext headers. Logging: implement structured logging using zerolog with trace_id and span_id fields in every log entry, define log levels and a policy for what must be logged at ERROR vs WARN vs INFO in a production service. Show how all three signals converge in a Grafana dashboard to answer: 'Why did checkout latency spike at 14:32?'","tags":["architecture","observability","golang","system-design","monitoring"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-90","title":"Domain-Driven Design Bounded Contexts","prompt":"You are a software architect applying Domain-Driven Design to a growing e-commerce monolith that the team is planning to decompose. The domain experts have been interviewed and identified significant language conflicts: the word 'product' means a physical SKU in the Catalog team's language, but means a purchased line item in the Billing team's language, and means a shippable unit with dimensions in the Fulfillment team's language. Apply DDD bounded context analysis to this domain. Your deliverable must include: a context map identifying at least 4 bounded contexts (Catalog, Ordering, Billing, Fulfillment) with their ubiquitous language glossary, the relationships between contexts (Customer-Supplier, Conformist, Anti-Corruption Layer) drawn as an ASCII context map, the anti-corruption layer pattern implemented in Python 3.12 showing how the Fulfillment context translates a CatalogProduct into a FulfillmentItem without polluting its domain model with Catalog concepts, an example of an aggregate root (Order) with its invariants enforced in the domain layer, and a migration roadmap from the current monolith to bounded-context-aligned modules over 6 months.","tags":["architecture","ddd","system-design","bounded-contexts","backend"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-91","title":"Explain Async/Await to a Junior Developer","prompt":"You are a senior JavaScript engineer mentoring a recent bootcamp graduate who understands synchronous code but is confused by async/await and keeps writing code that accidentally runs in parallel when it should be sequential. Write a mentoring document that starts with the callback pyramid-of-doom problem, evolves through Promises, and arrives at async/await as the natural solution. Your document must include: a concrete analogy (ordering coffee at a cafe — you don't block the cashier queue while waiting for your coffee), the callback version of fetching a user then fetching their orders then sending a notification, the same code progressively rewritten to Promises then async/await, a visual timeline diagram in ASCII art showing how the event loop continues processing other tasks while awaiting, the three most common mistakes juniors make (not awaiting, catching errors in the wrong place, accidentally sequential awaits when Promise.all would be 3x faster), and a cheat sheet of the 5 async patterns they will use every day (await, try/catch, Promise.all, Promise.allSettled, for-await-of).","tags":["learning","javascript","async","mentoring","teaching"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-92","title":"Write API Reference Documentation","prompt":"You are a developer advocate writing the official API reference documentation for a REST API endpoint that creates a webhook subscription in a SaaS platform. The endpoint is POST /v1/webhooks and requires authentication via Bearer token. Write the complete reference documentation page following Stripe's documentation quality standard. Your documentation must include: a one-sentence description of what the endpoint does and when a developer would use it, the complete HTTP request specification (method, URL, required headers), a parameters table with columns: name, type, required/optional, constraints, and description — covering url (HTTPS only), events (array of event type strings, 1–20 items), secret (auto-generated if omitted), and is_active (boolean, defaults to true), a Request example in curl showing all required and optional parameters, a Response example showing the 201 Created body with all fields, an Errors table covering 400 invalid URL format, 401 unauthorized, 409 duplicate URL, and 422 invalid event type, and a code example in Python 3.12 using the requests library showing the full integration pattern a developer would copy-paste.","tags":["documentation","api","developer-experience","writing","technical-writing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-93","title":"Architecture Decision Record Template","prompt":"You are a principal engineer establishing an Architecture Decision Record (ADR) process for a 30-engineer organization that currently makes major technical decisions in Slack threads that are lost within weeks. Write a complete ADR for the decision to adopt PostgreSQL as the standard relational database instead of MySQL for all new services — demonstrating the ADR format the team will use going forward. Your ADR must include: the standard header fields (title, date, status, deciders, context), a context section explaining the current situation (5 services use MySQL, 3 use PostgreSQL, inconsistency causes operational overhead), a decision statement written as a present-tense active decision (not a recommendation), a consequences section covering positive consequences (unified operational tooling, single DBA knowledge base), negative consequences (migration cost for 5 existing MySQL services), and risks (PostgreSQL expertise gap in 2 teams), a considered alternatives section covering MySQL (current standard) and Amazon Aurora with pros/cons for each, and a follow-up actions section with owners and due dates. Explain how to store ADRs in a git repository using the adr-tools CLI.","tags":["documentation","architecture","adr","decision-making","process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-94","title":"Onboarding Runbook for a New Backend Engineer","prompt":"You are a senior engineer writing a day-one onboarding runbook for a new mid-level backend engineer joining a team that builds a Node.js 20 + TypeScript 5 + PostgreSQL 15 API on Kubernetes. The new hire has 3 years of experience but has never used this specific stack. Write a complete onboarding document covering: a mental model section explaining how the three-tier architecture (API → Service Layer → Repository Layer) maps to the directory structure, the 10 CLI commands they will run every day (npm run dev, npm test, docker compose up, kubectl get pods, psql for database inspection), how to make their first code change — adding a new field to the User API response, including the migration, model change, serializer update, and test — as a guided walkthrough, the three most important team conventions (commit message format, PR size limits, error handling patterns), a debugging guide for the three most common local dev errors (port already in use, database migration pending, Docker volume permission error), and a glossary of the 15 internal acronyms and project names they will hear in their first week.","tags":["documentation","onboarding","node.js","kubernetes","team-process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-95","title":"Code Comment Best Practices Guide","prompt":"You are a senior engineer writing a code commenting style guide for a Python 3.12 team of 8 engineers who range from junior to senior. The team has two problems: junior engineers over-comment obvious code ('increment i by 1'), and senior engineers under-comment complex business logic assuming context that newcomers don't have. Write a practical style guide that teaches the team the difference between good and bad comments. Your guide must include: the golden rule (comments explain WHY not WHAT — the code says what, comments say why), 5 before/after examples of bad comments replaced with good ones (a magic number with business context, a non-obvious regex with an explanation of what domain rule it enforces, a performance optimization with a link to the benchmark, a workaround for a third-party library bug with a JIRA link and expected fix date, a complex algorithm with a link to the paper it implements), Google-style Python docstring format for public functions with Args, Returns, Raises, and Example sections, the TODO/FIXME/HACK comment convention with required ticket numbers, and a pre-commit hook script that flags TODO comments without a ticket reference.","tags":["documentation","code-comments","python","style-guide","best-practices"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-96","title":"Explain Docker and Containers to a Non-Engineer","prompt":"You are a senior DevOps engineer explaining Docker and containers to a product manager who needs to understand why the engineering team needs 2 weeks to containerize the legacy application. The PM has no terminal experience but understands the concept of software running on computers. Write a conceptual explanation using only real-world analogies. Your explanation must cover: the 'works on my machine' problem using the analogy of a recipe that assumes the cook has specific ingredients that aren't listed, what a container is using the shipping container analogy (standardized box that carries anything and works with any ship, port, or truck), what Docker is (the tool that builds and runs containers — like a factory that creates the shipping containers), the difference between a Docker image and a running container (like the difference between a recipe and a prepared meal), why containerization takes 2 weeks (porting assumptions about the server environment into explicit configuration, especially filesystem paths and environment variables), and three business benefits the PM can communicate to stakeholders (faster developer onboarding from days to hours, identical staging and production environments reducing release risk, independent scaling of individual services).","tags":["learning","docker","communication","non-technical","explanation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-97","title":"System Design Interview — URL Shortener","prompt":"You are a senior engineer coaching a candidate preparing for a staff engineer system design interview at a top tech company next week. Walk through the design of a URL shortener service (like bit.ly) that must handle 100 million URLs and 10 billion redirects per month. Structure your walkthrough as a live interview coaching session. Your coaching must cover: requirements clarification questions the candidate should ask first (read vs write ratio, URL expiry, analytics, custom slugs), back-of-the-envelope estimation showing 100M writes/month = 40 writes/second and 10B reads/month = 4,000 reads/second (read-heavy), the base62 encoding approach for generating 7-character short codes that gives 62^7 = 3.5 trillion unique codes, the database schema choice (PostgreSQL for writes with a Redis read-through cache for 99% of redirect traffic), the single-host bottleneck solution using distributed ID generation with Twitter Snowflake IDs, a complete architecture diagram in ASCII art with load balancer, API servers, Redis cluster, PostgreSQL with read replicas, and CDN, and the top 3 follow-up questions interviewers ask (how to handle custom vanity URLs, how to implement analytics, how to expire URLs).","tags":["learning","system-design","interview-prep","architecture","teaching"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-98","title":"Git Workflow Guide for a New Team","prompt":"You are a senior engineer writing a Git workflow guide for a newly formed team of 6 engineers who have varying levels of Git experience. Some engineers use Git only for committing and pushing; none have used a structured branching strategy before. Write a practical Git workflow guide based on trunk-based development with short-lived feature branches. Your guide must include: a diagram of the branching strategy (main is always deployable, feature branches live for less than 2 days, no long-lived develop or release branches), the 7 daily Git commands with annotated examples (git pull --rebase, git switch -c feature/JIRA-123-add-payment-method, git add -p for interactive staging, git commit with conventional commit format, git push --force-with-lease instead of --force, git rebase main, git log --oneline --graph), the pull request checklist the team must follow before requesting review, the three most destructive Git mistakes and how to recover from them (force-pushed over teammate's work, committed secrets, accidentally deleted a branch), a .gitconfig snippet with useful aliases, and a pre-commit hook configuration using Husky that runs ESLint and the test suite before every commit.","tags":["learning","git","workflow","team-process","version-control"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-99","title":"Explain the CAP Theorem with Real Examples","prompt":"You are a distributed systems educator writing a deep-dive explainer on the CAP Theorem for a senior engineer who has seen the acronym but doesn't have a solid mental model for applying it to architectural decisions. Your explanation must go beyond the textbook definition and build genuine intuition. Cover: the three properties defined concretely — Consistency (every read returns the most recent write or an error, not a stale value), Availability (every request receives a non-error response, even if it might be stale), and Partition Tolerance (the system continues operating when network messages are dropped between nodes), the fundamental insight that network partitions are not optional in any distributed system running on real networks (you only choose between C and A during a partition), three real production systems and their concrete CAP choices: PostgreSQL with synchronous replication (CP — it returns an error rather than serving stale data), Cassandra (AP — it serves the locally available replica even if it might be stale), and DynamoDB with eventual consistency reads (AP) vs strongly consistent reads (CP and slower), the PACELC extension that says even without a partition you still trade off latency vs consistency, and a decision checklist that an architect can apply to choose CP vs AP for a new service.","tags":["learning","distributed-systems","cap-theorem","architecture","teaching"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"c-100","title":"Write a Production Incident Postmortem","prompt":"You are a senior SRE writing a blameless postmortem document for a 47-minute production outage that affected 12,000 users of a payment processing SaaS. The incident: a database migration that added a NOT NULL column to a 200-million-row table caused a full table lock, the monitoring alert fired 8 minutes after the outage began because the alert threshold was too high, and the on-call engineer rolled back the migration after 39 more minutes. Write a complete postmortem following Google's SRE postmortem culture principles. Your postmortem must include: an executive summary (3 sentences: what happened, user impact, what was done), a timeline in UTC with each action timestamped to the minute (deploy started, first user impact, alert fired, on-call paged, investigation started, root cause identified, rollback initiated, service recovered), a root cause analysis section using the 5 Whys technique tracing from 'users got 500 errors' back to 'the migration strategy was not reviewed for large tables', a contributing factors section listing 3 factors without blaming individuals (missing staging data volume, alert threshold misconfigured, no runbook for migration rollback), and 5 concrete action items with owners, due dates, and success metrics (not 'improve monitoring' but 'reduce p99 alert threshold for checkout endpoint from 10s to 2s — owner: @alice, due: 2024-03-01, metric: alert fires within 2 minutes of next incident').","tags":["documentation","incident-response","sre","postmortem","process"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:15:00Z","featured":false}

];
  __exports.codingPrompts = codingPrompts;
})();
